-- title:
--   Computing the size of a compact subset downset
-- statement:
--   For natural numbers $c\le L$, the compact downset contains all subsets of an $L$-element ground set with size at most $c$. Its number of cells is
--   $$K(L,c)=\sum_{i=0}^{c}\binom Li.$$
--   Suppose the program contains the specified setup and layer-offset procedures. With scratch base $w$ and machine limits $S,W,D$, assume
--   $$w+(L+1)^2+(L+1)\le S\le W,\quad(L+1)2^{L+1}\le W,\quad d+L+4\le D.$$
--   The procedure returns $K(L,c)$ within $23L^2+286L+314$ steps, from any initial memory, and preserves all cells below $w$.
--
--   This computes the exact stride of a stored downset table instead of requiring a caller-supplied table length.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/LightDownsetSize.lean#L30-L82

